          "label": "Linear factor from difference of cubes",
          "inputProfile": "expression",
          "expected": "x-{{n}}"
        },
        {
          "id": "quadratic-factor",
          "label": "Quadratic factor from difference of cubes",
          "inputProfile": "expression",
          "expected": "x^2+{{n}}*x+{{n2}}"
        }
      ],
      "solutionReview": {
        "headline": "Grouping can expose a cubic that then uses a cube identity.",
        "reasoning": [
          "Group x^3(x+{{c}})-{{n3}}(x+{{c}}) to get (x+{{c}})(x^3-{{n3}}).",
          "Then x^3-{{n}}^3=(x-{{n}})(x^2+{{n}}x+{{n2}}).",
          "The quartic therefore has two linear factors and one quadratic factor."
        ],
        "answerSummary": "Linear factors x+{{c}} and x-{{n}}; quadratic factor x^2+{{n}}x+{{n2}}."
      },
      "attemptFeedback": [
        "Complete the grouping step first; then inspect the remaining cubic for a special pattern."
      ],
      "supportHints": [
        "Group terms to expose a shared factor first, then inspect the remaining lower-degree expression for a standard factoring identity."
      ],
